NPU per-dispatch floor, measured in isolation -- Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G)
2026-09-07. env: mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2) + XRT SDK 2.21.75, native Windows.
Command: python3 kernels/dispatch_floor/measure_floor.py --iters 50 --warmup 5 \
           --sizes 1024,4096,16384,65536,262144,1048576,4194304

WHY: kernels/groupnorm_bf16 and kernels/attention_bf16 both lost to the Zen4 CPU,
and both write-ups blame a per-dispatch fixed cost of '~185-200us'. That constant
was inherited from a single 96KB probe during the GroupNorm work and never measured
on its own, yet every isolated-op verdict in this repo rests on it.

DESIGN: no compute tile at all -- shim -> memtile -> shim via ObjectFifo.forward(),
the structure of mlir-aie's basic/passthrough_dmas. No kernel math exists to
attribute time to, so per-call time is dispatch + DMA and nothing else. Every
payload is verified (output == input) before its timings are kept; compile is
excluded (warmup + @iron.jit kernel cache), so the timed loop recompiles nothing.

TWO LAYERS ARE TIMED PER CALL:
  wall = perf_counter around the @iron.jit call -- what a design actually pays.
  hw   = the runtime's OWN bracket around kernel(3,insts,n,*bufs) + wait(), returned
         per call as KernelResult.npu_time (ns). See xrtruntime/hostruntime.py run().
'host' is wall-hw: IRON's per-call host-side cost. Read that carefully -- the hw
bracket is NARROW. It opens only after the hw_context cache lookup, the pyxrt kernel-
handle retrieval and any insts_bo / buffer-coherence work, so all of that lands in the
host column despite being XRT/driver time rather than interpreter time. A cProfile of
this path (50 calls, N=4096) puts its largest entry at XRTRuntime.load with tottime ==
cumtime -- time inside a C call the profiler cannot see into -- and cProfile's own
overhead is ~0.36 ms/call here (0.98 ms/call profiled vs 0.62 unprofiled), so tracked
Python work is only on the order of ~100us/call. The stat/realpath/signature churn
(nt.stat x8, nt._getfinalpathname x4, inspect._signature_from_callable x6 PER LAUNCH,
from the artifacts_present check in callabledesign.py and load() in
xrtruntime/hostruntime.py:721) is real and worth hoisting, but it is the minority of
the 447us. So: host-side, MOSTLY XRT OUTSIDE THE SUBMIT BRACKET, not Python overhead.
How much is actually recoverable is untested -- a C++ host or a cached-handle re-submit
path is what would measure it.

NPU per-dispatch floor -- no-compute passthrough (shim->memtile->shim)
iters=50 warmup=5 (compile excluded, kernel cached)

   elems  KB moved   wall ms  wall min     hw ms    hw min   host ms  ok
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    1024       8.0    0.6557    0.5900    0.1780    0.1586    0.4778  yes
    4096      32.0    0.6441    0.5865    0.1765    0.1574    0.4676  yes
   16384     128.0    0.6100    0.5827    0.1773    0.1658    0.4327  yes
   65536     512.0    0.6827    0.5996    0.2092    0.1929    0.4735  yes
  262144    2048.0    0.7468    0.7204    0.3169    0.3069    0.4299  yes
 1048576    8192.0    1.2649    1.1757    0.7699    0.7461    0.4950  yes
 4194304   32768.0    3.3703    3.0740    2.6080    2.5632    0.7623  yes

WALL (what a design pays today):  time_ms = intercept + slope * bytes   (R^2 = 0.9990)
  intercept (FIXED PER-DISPATCH COST) : 617.0 us
  slope                               : 0.0817 ns/byte
  implied streaming bandwidth         : 12.24 GB/s

HARDWARE ONLY (submit+wait, no Python):  time_ms = intercept + slope * bytes   (R^2 = 1.0000)
  intercept (FIXED PER-DISPATCH COST) : 169.8 us
  slope                               : 0.0726 ns/byte
  implied streaming bandwidth         : 13.78 GB/s

Go/no-go threshold this implies:
  An op whose CPU time is under 617.0 us cannot win through
  the IRON call path regardless of kernel quality.
  Of that, 169.8 us is hardware and 447.3 us is host-side overhead that a
  leaner submission path could in principle remove.
  Compare against the measured CPU times before writing a kernel:
    attention_bf16 stage 4: CPU 12 us   stage 3: CPU 34 us   stage 2: CPU 240 us

=== WHAT THIS SETTLES ===

1. THE REPO'S "~185-200us" CONSTANT WAS RIGHT -- ABOUT THE HARDWARE. The measured
   hardware floor is 169.8us, squarely in that range. This is a confirmation, not a
   retraction. What was never separated is that a kernel does not PAY the hardware
   floor: through the @iron.jit path it pays 617.0us, i.e. 3.6x the number the
   write-ups were quoting. Both kernels/groupnorm_bf16 and kernels/attention_bf16
   were charged the wall figure while their analyses reasoned with the hardware one.

2. DISPATCH DOMINATES EVERYTHING BELOW ~0.5 MB. Wall time is flat from 8KB to 512KB
   (0.61-0.68 ms across a 64x payload range). Transfer only becomes visible past
   ~2 MB. Below that, payload size is not what a kernel is paying for.

3. GO/NO-GO THRESHOLD, to run BEFORE writing a kernel rather than after:
     through IRON today      : the op's CPU time must exceed ~617us
     best case, raw resubmit : the op's CPU time must exceed ~170us
   Both are LOWER BOUNDS for a real design: this is a no-compute passthrough, and a
   multi-core kernel's own configuration cost lands inside the hw bracket and pushes
   its floor above 169.8us. One design, one data point -- treat as a floor, not a
   universal constant.

4. IT CLOSES THE ATTENTION THREAD PROPERLY. Applying the measured floor to
   kernels/attention_bf16's own numbers, assuming a PERFECT kernel running at the
   895 GFLOPS this machine actually reached (mlir_aie_bf16_matmul_npu.log):

     stage   FLOP      compute@895  +617 wall   vs CPU     +170 hw     vs CPU
     ------  --------  -----------  ----------  ---------  ----------  --------
     2       36.18 M     40.4 us     657 us     240 us     210 us      240 us
                                     loses 2.7x            wins 1.14x (a wash)
     3        2.79 M      3.1 us     620 us      34 us     173 us       34 us
                                     loses 18x             loses 5.1x
     4        0.21 M      0.2 us     617 us      12 us     170 us       12 us
                                     loses 51x             loses 14x

   So the attention verdict SURVIVES the correction: even a perfect kernel on a
   perfect submit path only reaches a wash at stage 2 and still loses at 3 and 4.
   But the recorded DIAGNOSIS was wrong. The write-ups say the wall was per-dispatch
   fixed cost; at stage 2 dispatch is 617us of a measured 57,610us -- ~1%. The real
   cause is kernel design: attention_kernels.cc contains ZERO uses of aie::mmul and
   instead hand-rolls dot products with a horizontal aie::reduce_add PER OUTPUT
   ELEMENT, achieving 0.63 GFLOPS on hardware measured at 895. Row-wise streaming
   fixed the 128KB scratchpad overflow and destroyed the arithmetic intensity in the
   same edit. Do NOT reuse that inner loop as a template.

   Cross-check that the floor is consistent with that design: attention stage 4
   measured 860us wall for 0.21 MFLOP; 860 - 617 = 243us of compute + 8-core config,
   which is the right order for a kernel doing almost no math. The floor explains the
   fixed part of the attention numbers without being the reason they lost.

5. WHAT IT OPENS. 447.3us/call of host-side cost sits outside the hardware bracket and
   is flat in payload size. How much is recoverable is UNMEASURED -- a cached-handle
   re-submit, a C++ host, or a batched/chained submit (xrt::runlist, if this XRT
   exposes it) are the candidates. If that cost were eliminated the go/no-go threshold
   drops 3.6x, which would not save attention but would move the boundary for every
   future kernel. That is the next measurement, not a claim.

=== FOLLOW-UP: THE BATCHED-SUBMIT PATH EXISTS (verified, not yet measured) ===
Checked directly on this machine while writing the above:

  xrt::runlist  -> present in /c/Xilinx/XRT/xrt_sdk/xrt/include/xrt/experimental/xrt_kernel.h
                   (experimental namespace in XRT 2.21.75, not stable API)
  pyxrt.runlist -> BOUND in this install's pyxrt, exposing add / execute / wait
                   (python3 -c "import pyxrt; dir(pyxrt.runlist)")

So N dispatches can in principle be queued with add(), submitted with a single execute()
and joined with one wait(). That attacks BOTH terms measured above: the 447.3us host cost
would be paid once per batch instead of once per call, and the 169.8us hardware floor may
partly amortize as well since it covers submit+wait.

This is an API-existence check ONLY. Nothing here has been run: no measurement, no claim
about what it actually saves, and "experimental" means the signature can move between XRT
versions. It is recorded as the next measurement to make, not as a result.
